tdemucs_6s` guitar (experimental, bleeding — LALAL covers it) |\n./docs/phase1-song-prep-plan.md:94:  \"stems\":      {\"backend\": \"lalal\", \"model\": \"...\", \"files\": {\"guitar\": \"stems/guitar.wav\", \"...\": \"...\"}},\n./docs/phase1-song-prep-plan.md:120:Python 3.11 · `uv` for env/deps · `typer` CLI · `pydantic` manifest models · ffmpeg · LALAL.AI REST API (default separator) · torch (Demucs fallback; MPS) · ONNX Runtime (Basic Pitch) · Lua ReaScript with a vendored JSON decoder for the two REAPER actions.\n./docs/phase1-song-prep-plan.md:122:Target machine: **M4 MacBook Air, 16 GB unified memory** (confirmed). Two consequences: it's fanless, so sustained local separation would thermal-throttle — another reason LALAL-by-default is right; and with separation offloaded, the remaining local stages (madmom, chord CNN, Basic Pitch) are lightweight, putting expected end-to-end time at ~2–3 min/song plus upload. Fully offline operation (Demucs fallback) remains supported, just slower and warmer.\n./docs/phase1-song-prep-plan.md:129:| M1 | Separation stage: LALAL adapter (default) + Demucs fallback behind one `Separator` interface | guitar/bass/drums/vocals(/other) stems land in the song folder from either backend; stage cached so each song costs credits once | M |\n./docs/phase1-song-prep-plan.md:143:- **Cloud in the default path**: LALAL API changes, credit exhaustion, or subscription lapse must degrade gracefully to Demucs — the fallback is exercised in tests, not just theoretical. Uploaded audio is the user's own, for personal practice.\n./docs/phase1-song-prep-plan.md:149:1. ~~Target machine?~~ Resolved: M4 MacBook Air, 16 GB. LALAL default; Demucs fallback on MPS.\n./docs/phase1-song-prep-plan.md:152:4. ~~LALAL.AI subscription?~~ Resolved: active — API adapter is the default separator, built in M1.\n./docs/phase1-song-prep-plan.md:156:On the target machine: open an existing REAPER project containing a song item → run `vgt: Analyze selected item` → pipeline completes (LALAL stems + local analysis) → `vgt: Apply results` augments the project per the tables above, non-destructively and idempotently; corrections in `corrections.yaml` survive re-runs; all analysis provenance is in `manifest.json`. Then phase 2 (practice controller: stem muting, loop/tempo management, take recording) builds on the same manifest and the same `[vgt]` track conventions.\n./docs/GOAL.md:83:Separate the reference track into practice stems using **LALAL.AI as the sole backend** — offline separation\n./docs/GOAL.md:86:deliberate cascade for the guitarless practice bed) and loading them as `[vgt]` tracks. The full design — LALAL\n./docs/GOAL.md:91:- **a LALAL v1 backend** behind a thin `Separator` seam, authenticated only by the `LALAL_LICENSE_KEY`\n./docs/GOAL.md:92:  environment variable (never persisted or logged), with idempotency-keyed, durably-checkpointed operations so\n./docs/GOAL.md:98:  requires an explicit `--force-stems` with a cost preview and confirmation.\n./docs/stem-separation-plan.md:6:in the `llm-wiki` vault; the LALAL.AI REST API v1\n./docs/stem-separation-plan.md:7:(`https://www.lalal.ai/api/v1/docs/`, OpenAPI specification at\n./docs/stem-separation-plan.md:8:`https://www.lalal.ai/api/v1/openapi.json`); and vgt's existing sidecar /\n./docs/stem-separation-plan.md:16:**Scope decision (2026-07-20): LALAL.AI is the sole separation backend.**\n./docs/stem-separation-plan.md:23:manual LALAL workflow exactly — **no instrument probing, no configurable stem\n./docs/stem-separation-plan.md:53:- LALAL.AI is the cloud default; it exposes dedicated **acoustic-** and\n./docs/stem-separation-plan.md:55:  for a practice workflow. This is the decisive reason to build on LALAL.\n./docs/stem-separation-plan.md:65:- Resilience caveat: LALAL is subscription-gated. With no offline fallback,\n./docs/stem-separation-plan.md:70:**From LALAL API v1:**\n./docs/stem-separation-plan.md:78:  `POST /api/v1/check/` with task ids (→ progress %, then typed `s